class:: RIRFDN
summary:: Editable stable late field fitted from a measured room response
categories:: UGens>Reverbs, Libraries>CamaraObscura
related:: Classes/DarkVelvetReverb, Classes/GeometryReverb, Classes/GroupedFDN, Classes/ModalPlate, Classes/ModalReverbBank, Classes/RIRFDN, Classes/VelvetFDN, Guides/CamaraObscura

description::

image::../Guides/ir_rirfdn.png#RIRFDN shows an instantly smooth and dense exponential decay modeled directly from the Schroeder integration of a real space impulse response.::

Conceptually, `RIRFDN` acts as a bridge between the realism of convolution reverbs and the flexibility of algorithmic reverbs. While traditional convolution exactly reproduces a recorded Room Impulse Response (RIR) but cannot be easily altered, this reverb extracts the statistical decay characteristics of a real room and synthesizes them using a flexible algorithmic structure, allowing you to tweak the "real" room in ways convolution cannot.

Technically, the system utilizes a deterministic offline Schroeder integration analysis to extract frequency-dependent decay rates (T60) from an impulse response. `RIRFDN` then uses these extracted parameters to tune a highly optimized Feedback Delay Network (FDN). This allows the UGen to capture the late-field spectral signature of a measured space with a fraction of the CPU and memory cost of full convolution, while providing the ability to freeze, modulate, or infinitely extend the tail.

RIRFDN renders a prepared compact FDN fitted to the late part of an impulse
response. Analysis is external: the calculation callback sees only validated
delay, damping, residue, and matrix values. This first renderer reproduces
broad decay and two-band coloration, not the original early reflections,
fine comb response, spatial cues, or exact waveform.

Output is stereo wet audio. An optional compatible morph model runs as a
complete second stable network; outputs are crossfaded, avoiding unsafe raw
coefficient interpolation. Compatibility requires the same sample rate, line
count, topology fields, and channel count. Two networks approximately double
CPU and delay memory even when code::morph:: is zero.

Freeze suppresses excitation and approaches a stable per-delay gain of
0.999999. Reset clears both networks. The closest vanilla UGen is
link::Classes/PartConv::; RIRFDN is editable and compact, but deliberately
less exact than literal measured convolution.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: input
Mono audio input.

argument:: modelBuf
Loaded primary link::Classes/RIRFDNModel::.

argument:: morphBuf
Loaded compatible secondary model or -1 for no second network.

argument:: morph
Output crossfade from primary at 0 to secondary at 1.

argument:: decayScale
Stable T60 multiplier clipped to 0.05 through 20.

argument:: tone
Low/high decay tilt from -2 through 2.

argument:: freeze
Stable near-lossless hold and excitation suppression.

argument:: reset
Rising edge clears both recursive networks.

returns::
Stereo wet audio Array.

method:: kr
Unavailable; audio-rate only.

examples::

The following examples have been structured into individual blocks so you can easily run them step-by-step.

code::
// Boot the server first!
s.boot;

// 1. Prepare the model and source material
(
// Use a fallback to ExampleFiles.child if the external sample bundle is not installed
~path = Platform.userExtensionDir +/+ "CamaraObscura/sounds/";
~load = { |name| if(File.exists(~path +/+ name)) { ~path +/+ name } { ExampleFiles.child } };

~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("vocal.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("drum1.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("drum2.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("guitar.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("violin.wav"), channels: [0]);

~model = RIRFDNModel.synthetic(sampleRate: s.sampleRate).load(s);
)

// 2. Play the reverb (wait a moment for the buffers to load before executing this)
(
x = {
    var input = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~source, BufRateScale.kr(~source), loop: 1);
    // var input = SoundIn.ar(0); // alternative live input
    
    var fade = EnvGen.kr(Env([0, 1], [0.12]));
    var wet = RIRFDN.ar(input, ~model)[0];
    
    (input + wet) * fade * 0.25 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
x.free;
~source.free;
~model.free;
::

// Creative Example: Infinite freeze via feedback modulation
code::
// 1. Load the model
~creativeModel = ~creativeModel = RIRFDNModel.synthetic(sampleRate: s.sampleRate).load(s);

// 2. Play
(
y = {
    var freezeEnv = LFPulse.kr(0.2, 0, 0.5).range(1.0, 1.1);
    RIRFDN.ar(Dust.ar(5), ~creativeModel, t60Mult: freezeEnv)[0]
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
y.free;
~creativeModel.free;
::
